# PHP / Laravel testing

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic testing rules to Laravel's
testing stack — Pest (the current default for new Laravel apps) or
PHPUnit, plus Laravel's HTTP/database testing helpers. Applies only to
`*.php` files.

## Layout and naming

- Tests live under `tests/Feature` (anything touching the framework —
  HTTP, database, queue, auth) or `tests/Unit` (a class tested in
  isolation, no framework boot needed); match whichever the project
  already uses for a given kind of test rather than defaulting one way.
- Pest: a test file is a plain script of `test('...', function () {...})`/
  `it('...', function () {...})` calls, optionally grouped with
  `describe(...)`. PHPUnit: a test class extends `Tests\TestCase`, with
  each test a `public function test_...(): void` method or a method
  annotated `#[Test]`.
- Name a test for the behavior it verifies ("rejects an order with no
  line items"), not the method under test alone ("test_store").

## Database and factories

- Use model factories (`app/Models/*` + `database/factories/*Factory.php`,
  `User::factory()->create([...])`) to build test data instead of
  hand-inserting rows; override only the fields the test cares about.
- Apply `RefreshDatabase` (or `DatabaseTransactions`, on a project already
  using it) on any test that touches the database, so each test starts
  from a known, isolated state — do not rely on execution order between
  tests for shared fixture data.

## HTTP and feature tests

- Exercise routes through Laravel's HTTP testing helpers
  (`$this->get(...)`, `$this->postJson(...)`, `actingAs($user)`) and
  assert on the response (`assertStatus`, `assertJson`, `assertSee`,
  `assertRedirect`) rather than calling controller methods directly —
  a feature test should exercise the same path a real request takes
  (routing, middleware, validation included).
- Assert authorization/validation failures explicitly
  (`assertForbidden()`, `assertInvalid(['field'])`) instead of only
  checking the happy path.

## Fakes over real side effects

- Use Laravel's fakes (`Queue::fake()`, `Mail::fake()`, `Event::fake()`,
  `Storage::fake('disk')`, `Http::fake([...])`) to assert a job was
  dispatched, a mail was queued, or an HTTP call was made — never let a
  test actually send an email, hit a real queue, or make a real outbound
  HTTP request.
- After `Queue::fake()`, assert with `Queue::assertPushed(JobClass::class,
  fn ($job) => ...)` to check the job's own state, not just that some job
  of that class was pushed.

## Assertions and determinism

- Never synchronize with `sleep()`/a fixed delay to wait for a queued job
  or async side effect in a test — use `Queue::fake()` and assert what
  was dispatched, or run the job synchronously in the test
  (`ShouldQueue` jobs can be dispatched and handled inline via
  `Bus::fake()`/direct `handle()` invocation) instead of waiting on a
  real worker.
- Prefer Pest's `expect($value)->toBe(...)` (or PHPUnit's
  `assertSame`) over a loose equality check when the type matters (`===`
  semantics), especially for IDs, enum cases, and money values.

## Static analysis and running

- Run the project's configured static analysis (`./vendor/bin/phpstan
  analyse`, typically via Larastan on a Laravel app) alongside the test
  suite when configured — a green test suite with new static-analysis
  errors is not a finished change.
- Run `php artisan test` (Pest or PHPUnit, whichever the project uses) or
  `./vendor/bin/pest`/`./vendor/bin/phpunit` directly; do not skip a slow
  test file to get a faster local loop without saying so in the report.
